hfs: pin the extent format constants the large-file audit relies on

BRING-UP-STATE 77 audits whether the HFS+ write path can create the
592,101,376-byte file the P1 install needs.  The writer is Apple's full
hfs-627.100.6 implementation, including the extents overflow B-tree, and
the audit's arithmetic assumes an 8-entry inline extent record, an
8-byte extent descriptor and a 10-byte extent key.

Pin those three format constants with compile-time assertions in the
KEBAB compat header.  A future source import that changes them will fail
the build instead of silently invalidating the audit.

No behavior change.

--- a/bsd/kebab_hfs/kebab_hfs_compat.h
+++ b/bsd/kebab_hfs/kebab_hfs_compat.h
@@ -23,6 +23,21 @@
 #include "core/kext-config.h"
 #include "hfs_encodings/hfs_encodings_internal.h"
 
+/*
+ * BRING-UP-STATE 77: the P1 install writes a 592,101,376-byte file through
+ * hfs_vnop_write.  The writer is Apple's full hfs-627.100.6 implementation,
+ * including the extents overflow B-tree, and the audit's arithmetic is tied
+ * to these on-disk format constants.  Fail the build if a future source
+ * import changes them, so the audit is redone instead of silently invalid.
+ */
+#include "core/hfs_format.h"
+_Static_assert(kHFSPlusExtentDensity == 8,
+    "HFS+ extent audit (BRING-UP-STATE 77) assumes 8 inline extents");
+_Static_assert(sizeof(HFSPlusExtentDescriptor) == 8,
+    "HFS+ extent descriptor is not the audited 8 bytes");
+_Static_assert(kHFSPlusExtentKeyMaximumLength == 10,
+    "HFS+ extent key is not the audited 10 bytes");
+
 /* iOS kext-config.h enables AppleKeyStore; this board has none. */
 #undef CONFIG_PROTECT
 #define CONFIG_PROTECT 0
